<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Lament · c. 1924 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fa" dir="rtl">مرغ سحر</bdi> / <bdi lang="fa-Latn">Morgh-e Sahar</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Persian song from the 1920s about a caged bird at dawn. Iranians have sung it for a hundred years to ask for freedom.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mohammad-Taqi Bahar</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Morteza Neidavoud</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Iranians; Iranian movements for constitutional government and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tehran, Iran</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 20th century, after the Iranian Constitutional Revolution; first sung in public 1924; first recorded 1927</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Mohammad-Taqi Bahar (1927; he died in 1951); music by Morteza Neydavud)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Morq-e Sahar&quot; is a tasnif, a Persian art song, with words by the poet Mohammad-Taqi Bahar and music by Morteza Neidavoud. It was made in the early 20th century in the wake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a>, and it has often been sung in protest against injustice, dictatorship and tyranny in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; it is often called the &quot;anthem&quot; of Iran's struggles for freedom.</p><p>The first singer to perform it was Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, who sang it in 1924 at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> hotel in her first formal concert. It was first recorded in 1927 by Iranoddole Helen (Taj Esfahani); one of the first recordings is also attributed to Moluk Zarrabi. The song has two stanzas. The first was described as the more lyrical and was often sung in public; the second, which deals with social and political questions, was sung at private parties, where talk turned more easily to politics. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah Pahlavi</a> heard the song performed at a gathering in the home of his minister Teymourtash, he banned the second stanza in 1925, and it stayed banned until his forced abdication in 1941.</p><p>Many Iranian singers have performed it since, among them Mohammad-Reza Shajarian, Homayoun Shajarian, Leila Forouhar, Farhad Mehrad, Shakila, Mohsen Namjoo, Reza Sadeghi and Hengameh Akhavan. It became the signature piece of Mohammad-Reza Shajarian, who was widely seen as a public voice for democracy and closed all his concerts with it. When Shajarian died in 2020, crowds of mourners gathered at the Jam Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, where he had been treated, sang &quot;Morq-e Sahar&quot; and chanted slogans against authoritarian rule, which led to a confrontation with the Iranian authorities.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of lament and of longing for freedom, built on the image of the dawn bird of its title. It was heard in its time as a call for liberty.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Persian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Persian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">مرغ سحر ناله سر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">Morgh-e sahar nāle sar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bird of the dawn, raise your lament,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">داغ مرا تازه‌تر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">dāgh-e marā tāze-tar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make my wound fresh again;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">ز آهِ شرربار این قفس را بَرشِکَن و زیر و زِبَر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">ze āh-e sharar-bār in qafas rā bar-shekan o zir-o-zebar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with a sigh that rains sparks, break this cage and turn it upside down.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">بلبلِ پَربسته ز کنجِ قفس درآ نغمهٔ آزادیِ نوع بشر سَرا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">bolbol-e par-baste ze konj-e qafas dar-ā naghme-ye āzādi-ye now'-e bashar sarā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nightingale with bound wings, come out of the corner of the cage, sing the song of the freedom of humankind,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">وَز نفسی عرصهٔ این خاک توده را پرشرر کن، پرشرر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">v-az nafasi 'arse-ye in khāk-tude rā por-sharar kon, por-sharar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and with one breath fill the field of this heap of dust with sparks, fill it with sparks.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">ظلم ظالم، جور صیاد آشیانم داده بر باد</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">zolm-e zālem, jowr-e sayyād āshiyānam dāde bar bād</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The tyranny of the tyrant, the cruelty of the hunter, have scattered my nest to the wind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">ای خدا، ای فلک، ای طبیعت شام تاریک ما را سحر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">ey Khodā, ey falak, ey tabi'at shām-e tārik-e mā rā sahar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O God, O heaven, O nature, turn our dark night into dawn.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">نوبهار است، گل به‌بار است ابر چشمم ژاله‌بار است</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">now-bahār ast, gol be-bār ast abr-e cheshmam zhāle-bār ast</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is early spring, the flowers are in bloom; the cloud of my eye rains dew;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">این قفس، چون دلم، تنگ و تار است</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">in qafas, chon delam, tang o tār ast</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>this cage, like my heart, is narrow and dark.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">شعله فکن در قفس ای آهِ آتشین</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">sho'le fekan dar qafas ey āh-e ātashin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cast flame into the cage, O fiery sigh;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">دستِ طبیعت گُلِ عمر مرا مچین</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">dast-e tabi'at gol-e 'omr-e marā machin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hand of nature, do not pluck the flower of my life;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">جانبِ عاشق نِگَه ای تازه گل از این بیشتر کن، بیشتر کن، بیشتر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">jāneb-e 'āsheq negah ey tāze gol az in bishtar kon, bishtar kon, bishtar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O fresh flower, look toward the lover more than this, more, more.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fa" dir="rtl">مرغ بی‌دل، شرح هجران مختصر، مختصر کن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="fa-Latn">morgh-e bidel, sharh-e hejrān mokhtasar, mokhtasar kon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heartsick bird, make the tale of separation short, make it short.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Mohammad-Taqi Bahar (1927): the first stanza, the one sung; as quoted in the Persian Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%BA_%D8%B3%D8%AD%D8%B1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC-style romanization of Persian, as pronounced in Iran).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>This is the first of Bahar's two stanzas, the one sung. The second, more openly political, names the betrayers of the constitution; it is printed in the Persian Wikipedia's article but rarely performed.</li><li>The bird in the cage is the nation under despotism; the song was first sung in 1927, in the last years of the Constitutional Revolution's struggle, and has been a song of protest in Iran ever since.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
